\par\par \b \uc2 Investors Are Using AI for Financial Decisions. They Still Want a Human Advisor. -- Barrons.com\b0\par\par\uc2 By Andrew Welsch \par \uc2 473 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2026\par 21:45\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Even as more investors use \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  to inform their financial and investment decisions, they still want a human touch.\par \par \uc2 That is one of several findings from a new survey commissioned by global bank \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , which found that many U.S. investors are embracing a hybrid approach that involves relying on AI for analysis and research, but turning to a human financial advisor for strategic expertise and reassurance.\par \par \uc2 "Clients are more informed and more confident because when they go into a meeting with their advisor they feel AI has given them the information to ask the right question," says Racquel Oden, head of international wealth management and private banking, U.S. at \uc2 HSBC\uc2 . "But trust still sits with the human being."\par \par \uc2 Investment ideas are also more likely to come from a human being than AI, according to the survey. Fifty-nine percent of U.S. respondents said their last investment idea came from a financial professional. That compares to 19% who cited AI.\par \par \uc2 Oden says advisors can also help investors cut through the noise. "We live in a society of data overload," she says. "You need a professional to help you navigate this problem. And all the data we are getting is adding to the complexity of our lives."\par \par \uc2 She adds, "Information is not advice."\par \par \uc2 Young investors are embracing AI to a far greater degree than older investors. The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  survey found that 63% of Gen Z and millennial investors say AI has boosted their confidence and decision-making compared with just 31% of Gen X and baby boomer investors.\par \par \uc2 There is also a divide between U.S. investors and their global peers: 57% of affluent investors in the U.S. report using AI for financial and investment tasks compared with 73% of affluent investors globally, according to the survey. Oden notes an interesting dichotomy: Many of the best known \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  companies are based in the U.S., but when it comes to using AI for financial and investment tasks, American investors are lagging behind their global peers.\par \par \uc2 The \uc2 HSBC\uc2  study surveyed about 10,000 affluent and high-net-worth individual investors in 10 markets: China, Hong Kong, India, Malaysia, Mexico, Singapore, Taiwan, the U.A.E., the U.K. and the U.S. It defines affluent investors as individuals with at least $100,000 in investible assets and high-net-worth investors as individuals with at least $2 million in investible assets.\par \par \uc2 Write to Andrew Welsch at andrew.welsch@barrons.com\par \par \uc2 This content was created by Barron's, which is operated by Dow Jones & Co. Barron's is published independently from Dow Jones Newswires and The Wall Street Journal.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2026 15:45 ET (19:45 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260624em6o002p0\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
