\par\par \b \uc2 Micron\uc2  Shares Jump as Chip Shortage Projected to Last Beyond 2027 -- 2nd Update\b0\par\par\uc2 By Bradley Olson and Elias Schisgall \par \uc2 536 \uc2 words\par \uc2 25 June 2026\par 00:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 Micron Technology\uc2 , which has seen its value triple this year, said revenue quadrupled in the second quarter and forecast that rapid growth will continue due to near-insatiable demand for its memory chips.\par \par \uc2 Shares surged by as much as 15% in after-hours trading after the company beat Wall Street expectations in virtually every financial metric watched closely by investors, including sales, earnings per share and adjusted gross margin. The company also raised its forecast.\par \par \uc2 Micron\uc2  and its peers make chips that have become a central component in the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  boom, helping to facilitate the high-powered computing needed by top AI models.\par \par \uc2 Management told investors the chip shortage would go beyond 2027, a significant change from previous guidance that called for it to end by the start of next year. The company also disclosed that it has struck 16 long-term deals to supply chips, a massive increase from the past.\par \par \uc2 Memory chip-making has long been a cyclical business driven by boom and bust cycles, so long-term deals have begun to signal to some investors that a new era has dawned.\par \par \uc2 "Supply shortages in memory and storage will take considerable time to improve," Chief Executive \uc2 Sanjay Mehrotra\uc2  said in the call. "Even as we expect industry supply to improve gradually in 2028, we currently do not have line of sight as to when memory supply will be able to catch up with increasing demand."\par \par \uc2 Supply growth depends on building new fabs, which face constraints from worker shortages, regulations and needs for new energy, he said.\par \par \uc2 Micron\uc2  on Wednesday reported a third-quarter profit of $28.24 billion, or $24.67 a share, compared with a profit of $1.89 billion, or $1.68 a share, a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Stripping out certain one-time items, \uc2 Micron\uc2  reported adjusted earnings of $25.11 a share. Analysts polled by FactSet were expecting $20.86 a share.\par \par \uc2 Revenue surged to $41.46 billion, up from $9.3 billion a year prior and beating analyst expectations of $35.91 billion.\par \par \uc2 Micron\uc2  has been reaping the benefits of an industrywide shortage of memory technology, an important component of \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  hardware. The company signed a long-term supply agreement with \uc2 Anthropic\uc2  and invested in the AI lab earlier this week, although financial terms weren't provided.\par \par \uc2 Before the earnings report, the stock had been hit by investor worries about runaway tech valuations, high AI capital spending, and possible interest-rate increases from the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Investors have generally favored chip makers throughout the course of the AI boom, but occasionally setbacks in expansion possibilities and threats from new players that need less intense computing capabilities have damped enthusiasm.\par \par \uc2 For the current fourth quarter, the company is projecting adjusted earnings between $30 and $32 a share on revenue between $49 billion and $51 billion.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect adjusted earnings of $25.72 a share on $43.58 billion in revenue.\par \par \uc2 Write to Bradley Olson at bradley.olson@wsj.com and Elias Schisgall at elias.schisgall@wsj.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2026 18:04 ET (22:04 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260624em6o003ee\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
