\par\par \b \uc2 Asian Morning Briefing: U.S. Stocks Mixed as Tech Selloff Continues\b0\par\par\uc2 1,536 \uc2 words\par \uc2 24 June 2026\par 22:50\par \uc2 Dow Jones Newswires\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 English\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2026, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 MARKET SNAPSHOT\par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks finished mixed as investors continued to cash out of speculative bets on \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  companies. Treasury yields extended their decline as crude prices fell around 4% and settled near pre-war levels, easing inflation fears. Gold prices tumbled, extending the previous session's losses, while the dollar strengthened.\par \par \uc2 MARKET WRAPS\par \par \uc2 EQUITIES\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock indexes pared early gains, with the Nasdaq composite falling 0.4% after already declining 4% over the past two days. The Dow industrials rose 0.4%. The S&P 500 drifted 0.1% lower.\par \par \uc2 AI remains very much the focus: Shares in \uc2 Cerebras Systems\uc2  sold off 20% after it warned it expects to keep operating at a loss, another reminder of the heavy costs of the AI buildout. The PHLX Semiconductor Index pared losses to finish 0.2% lower, following a decline of nearly 8% Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Despite recent volatility, South Korean AI chip maker \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  is testing investor appetite with plans for a massive $29 billion U.S. listing. Its U.S. rival Micron, reported a more than fourfold increase in third-quarter revenue to $41.46 billion, driven by strong chip demand.\par \par \uc2 Earlier Wednesday, Asian markets were choppy as investors re-examined technology stocks following a red-hot run fueled by the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  boom and recent easing of Middle East tensions.\par \par \uc2 South Korea's Kospi rose 3.3% after swinging between gains and losses, following the 10% correction in the previous session. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  jumped 9.8% and \uc2 SK Hynix\uc2  was up 1.0%.\par \par \uc2 China's Shanghai Composite Index edged 0.1% higher while the Shenzhen Composite Index added 0.8%. The tech-focused ChiNext Price Index gained 1.4%. Hong Kong's Hang Seng nudged 0.3% higher.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei Stock Average declined 0.9%, weighed by chip and insurance stocks. Opinions from the \uc2 Bank of Japan\uc2 's last meeting show a growing sense of worry about inflation and a need to lift interest rates at a steady pace to avoid falling behind the curve.\par \par \uc2 Stocks in Australia rose, as the S&P/ASX 200 added 0.2%. Australia's consumer price growth eased in May amid cooling fuel prices, but underlying inflation continued to strengthen as businesses passed on higher costs resulting from the Middle East conflict.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand's S&P/NZX 50 Index fell 0.3%.\par \par \uc2 COMMODITIES\par \par \uc2 Oil futures fell to their lowest level since the start of the U.S.-Iran conflict as more oil shipments made it out of the Persian Gulf.\par \par \uc2 Signs are that production and exports will return much faster than had been thought during the war, \uc2 Mizuho\uc2 's \uc2 Robert Yawger\uc2  said.\par \par \uc2 "Once storage draws down, oil producers can ramp up production and return to business as usual" as long as the U.S. and Iran reach an agreement in the 60-day negotiation period that keeps the Strait of Hormuz open.\par \par \uc2 Yawger expects the Trump administration would extend the negotiating period rather than go back on the offensive in mid-August, "just two-and-a-half months away from the mid-term elections, where affordability will be a major issue."\par \par \uc2 WTI sank 3.9% to settle at $70.34 a barrel and front-month Brent fell 4.3% to $73.74.\par \par \uc2 Front-month gold futures settled down 3.4% to $3,990.30 a troy ounce, its lowest close since early November, and its fourth consecutive losing session.\par \par \uc2 The main catalyst for gold selling came from Treasury Secretary Scott Bessent, who predicted a return to 3% GDP growth by year-end in a CNBC interview.\par \par \uc2 That would mean higher interest rates, said Peter Cardillo of Spartan Capital Securities. Cardillo added that additional downside pressure could come as a result of the close under $4,000.\par \par \uc2 Silver finished down 6.4% to $58.052 a troy ounce.\par \par \uc2 TODAY'S TOP HEADLINES\par \par \uc2 Trump, Frustrated With High Gas Prices, Channels Biden's Criticism of Big Oil\par \par \uc2 President Trump is pulling from a familiar Democratic playbook as he wrestles with sticky gasoline prices: blaming Big Oil.\par \par \uc2 During the U.S. war against Iran, Trump repeatedly promised that prices at the pump would fall quickly once the conflict ended. But 10 days after he announced an agreement with Iran, the price of a gallon of gasoline still hovers around $3.93-nearly $1 higher than before the hostilities broke out.\par \par \uc2 Now, Trump is accusing his own oil allies of gouging consumers. Late Tuesday, Trump said on Truth Social that big oil companies aren't dropping their price at the pump in a way that is commensurate with the sharply lower prices they are paying for crude-and he has instructed the Justice Department to investigate.\par \par \uc2 Big Banks Ace an Easier Annual Stress Test\par \par \uc2 JPMorgan Chase, Bank of America and a collection of their peers all passed the \uc2 Federal Reserve\uc2 's annual stress tests, a widely expected outcome after regulators last year began disclosing the exam's scenarios and models in advance.\par \par \uc2 The Fed on Wednesday said that big banks were equipped to weather severe economic headwinds. After putting banks through a hypothetical situation where a severe global recession leads to significant declines in commercial real estate and house prices and rising unemployment, bank capital fell only slightly, staying well above the minimum level at which the Fed considers banks to be safe.\par \par \uc2 As a group, the banks would have faced only $708 in total loan losses, the smallest total of any test in the last seven years.\par \par \uc2 Micron Revenue Soars on Continued Memory Demand\par \par \uc2 Micron Technology reported a more than fourfold increase in revenue in its latest quarter, boosted by continued strong demand for its memory chips.\par \par \uc2 The company on Wednesday reported a third-quarter profit of $28.24 billion, or $24.67 a share, compared with a profit of $1.89 billion, or $1.68 a share, a year earlier.\par \par \uc2 Stripping out certain one-time items, Micron reported adjusted earnings of $25.11 a share. Analysts polled by FactSet were expecting $20.86 a share.\par \par \uc2 New Drugs Are Replacing Chemo for Aggressive Breast Cancer\par \par \uc2 Drug companies have found promising new options for one of the most aggressive and hardest-to-treat forms of breast cancer after decades with few breakthroughs.\par \par \uc2 Gilead Sciences won Food and Drug Administration approval Wednesday to sell its drug Trodelvy as a first treatment for newly diagnosed patients with the advanced form of a type of breast cancer known as "triple negative" because it has characteristics that render common treatments ineffective.\par \par \uc2 It is the second such approval in about a month: AstraZeneca and Daiichi Sankyo's rival drug, Datroway, was approved for a similar group of patients in May.\par \par \uc2 OpenAI, Broadcom Develop Custom Chip for AI Inference\par \par \uc2 OpenAI and Broadcom collaborated to develop a custom \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  chip, marking the next step in the ChatGPT-parent's strategy to build out a full stack behind its models and products.\par \par \uc2 The chip, called Jalape\u241\'00\'F1o, was built from scratch specifically for large-language model inference, with the goal of improving efficiency and lowering the cost to serve AI systems at scale, the companies said Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 Early tests show that the chip will deliver performance per watt "substantially better" than current state-of-the-art chips, they added. OpenAI and Broadcom didn't disclose specific performance measures but said a more-detailed technical report will be presented in the coming months.\par \par \uc2 Memory-Chip Giant SK Hynix Seeks to Raise $29 Billion in U.S. Listing\par \par \uc2 South Korea's SK Hynix plans to raise more than $29 billion through a U.S. listing, seeking to tap foreign investors to fund its expansion plans as the \b \highlight22\uc2 artificial-intelligence\b0 \highlight\uc2  boom fuels a rush for advanced chips.\par \par \uc2 The world's second-largest memory-chip maker is looking to raise 45.453 trillion won, equivalent to $29.65 billion, by issuing American depositary receipts on the Nasdaq market, according to a regulatory filing to the Korea Exchange on Wednesday. The company expects trading to begin July 10.\par \par \uc2 The deal would be one of the biggest share sales in history, comparable to Saudi Aramco's 2019 initial public offering and surpassing Alibaba's U.S. ADR offering in 2014.\par \par \uc2 Expected Major Events for Thursday\par \par \uc2 01:30/AUS: May Labour Force\par \par \uc2 01:30/AUS: May Job Vacancies\par \par \uc2 05:00/JPN: Apr Indexes of Business Conditions - Revision\par \par \uc2 05:30/JPN: May Tokyo area department store sales\par \par \uc2 05:30/JPN: May Nationwide department store sales\par \par \uc2 08:30/HK: May External Merchandise Trade\par \par \uc2 10:59/THA: May Trade data\par \par \uc2 23:30/JPN: Jun CPI (Tokyo), CPI ex-Food (Tokyo)\par \par \uc2 23:50/JPN: Jun Provisional Trade Statistics for 1st 10 days of Month\par \par \uc2 All times in GMT. Powered by Onclusive and Dow Jones.\par \par \uc2 Write to us at singaporeeditors@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 We offer an enhanced version of this briefing that is optimized for viewing on mobile devices and sent directly to your email inbox. If you would like to sign up, please go to {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "https://newsplus.wsj.com/subscriptions"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 https://newsplus.wsj.com/subscriptions}}}\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 This article is a text version of a Wall Street Journal newsletter published earlier today.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 June 24, 2026 16:50 ET (20:50 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Document DJDN000020260624em6o0036f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
